In triangle $ABC$, where $A$, $B$, and $C$ are the angles opposite the sides of lengths $a$, $b$, and $c$ respectively, it is given that $a(\cos B + \cos C) = b + c$.

(1) Prove that $\angle A = 90^\circ$;

(2) If the radius of the circumcircle of $\triangle ABC$ is 1, find the range of the perimeter of $\triangle ABC$.

Given $α \in \left( \frac{\pi}{2}, \pi \right)$, and $\tan α = -2$.

$(1)$ Find the value of $\sin \left( \frac{\pi}{4} + α \right)$;

$(2)$ Find the value of $\cos \left( \frac{2\pi}{3} - 2α \right)$.

Given two vectors in the plane, $\overrightarrow {a}=(\cos\alpha，(\lambda-1)\sin\alpha)$ and $\overrightarrow {b}=(\cos\beta，\sin\beta)$, where $\lambda>0$ and $0<\alpha<\beta<\frac{\pi}{2}$, if the vector $\overrightarrow {a}+ \overrightarrow {b}$ is perpendicular to $\overrightarrow {a}- \overrightarrow {b}$,  
(1) find the value of the real number $\lambda$;  
(2) if $\overrightarrow {a}\cdot \overrightarrow {b}= \frac{4}{5}$ and $\tan\alpha= \frac{4}{3}$, find the value of $\alpha$ (express the result in terms of inverse trigonometric functions).

The hexagon with the R is colored red. Each hexagon is colored either red, blue, or green, such that no two hexagons with a common side are colored the same color. In how many different ways can the figure be colored?

[asy]
path a=(0,0)--(10,0)--(15,8.7)--(10,17.3)--(0,17.3)--(-5,8.7)--cycle;
draw(a);
draw(shift((15,8.7))*a);
draw(shift((15,-8.7))*a);
draw(shift((30,17.3))*a);
draw(shift((30,0))*a);
draw(shift((30,-17.3))*a);
draw(shift((45,26))*a);
draw(shift((45,8.7))*a);
draw(shift((45,-8.7))*a);
draw(shift((60,17.3))*a);
draw(shift((60,0))*a);
draw(shift((60,-17.3))*a);
draw(shift((75,8.7))*a);
draw(shift((75,-8.7))*a);
label("$R$",(5,10),S);
[/asy]

There are many fascinating connections between mathematics and literature. For example, there is the palindrome poem, such as "云边月影沙边雁，水外天光山外树", which if read backwards, becomes "树外山光天外水，雁边沙影月边云", offering a delightful sense and rhyme both ways! Similarly, mathematics features palindromic numbers, such as 88, 454, 7337, 43534, etc., which are the same whether read from left to right or right to left. Such numbers are known as "palindromic numbers," and reading them is quite interesting!
The two-digit palindromic numbers are 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, 99, making a total of 9;
The three-digit palindromic numbers include 101, 111, 121, 131, ..., 969, 979, 989, 999, making a total of 90;
So, how many five-digit palindromic numbers are there in total?

